1. Expain about Principles of Testing
Principles of Software Testing:

Software testing follows seven fundamental principles that help testers improve test effectiveness and avoid common mistakes.
These principles guide the testing process and ensure better software quality.

1. Testing Shows the Presence of Defects
Testing helps identify defects in the application, but it cannot prove that the software is completely free of bugs.
It only shows that defects exist, not that the product is perfect.

2. Exhaustive Testing is Impossible
It is not possible to test every input combination and scenario. Therefore, testers focus on risk-based testing and prioritize critical functionalities.

3. Early Testing Saves Time and Cost
Testing should begin as early as possible in the development lifecycle. Early defect detection reduces rework, saves money, and prevents major issues in later stages.

4. Defect Clustering
Most defects are usually found in a small number of modules. This means certain parts of the application are more error-prone and require more testing focus.

5. Pesticide Paradox
If the same test cases are executed repeatedly, they stop finding new defects. Test cases must be reviewed and updated regularly to identify new issues.

6. Testing is Context Dependent
Testing approaches vary based on the type of application. For example, testing a banking application is different from testing a gaming application.

7. Absence of Errors Fallacy
Even if software has no known defects, it may still fail if it does not meet user requirements or business needs.
A defect-free product is not useful if it does not satisfy customers.

2.Difference about QA and Dev Testing

Developer Testing is performed by developers to verify that individual components or modules of the application work correctly according to the technical requirements.
It mainly focuses on checking the correctness of the code, logic, and functionality at the unit and integration level. 
Developers usually write and execute unit tests and component tests during the development phase to detect defects early and ensure code quality.

QA Testing, on the other hand, is performed by Quality Assurance testers to validate the overall application from a user and business perspective. 
QA testing focuses on functional testing, usability testing, system testing, regression testing, and end-to-end validation. 
The main goal of QA testing is to ensure that the application meets business requirements, works correctly in real-world scenarios, and provides a good user experience.

3. Explain about STLC process.

STLC stands for Software Testing Life Cycle. It is a systematic process followed by the testing team to ensure that the software application is tested in a 
structured and efficient manner. STLC defines different testing phases, activities, and deliverables involved in testing.
